-- ALTER SETTINGS LANGUAGE ADD / REMOVE — the project's ENABLED languages.
--
-- The enabled list is what App Settings > Languages shows, and the only thing a
-- build emits translations for. Measured with `mxbuild --target=deploy`: a
-- caption translated into a language that is not enabled reaches NOTHING under
-- deployment/ — no translations_<code>.properties is produced at all — while the
-- model and Studio Pro both keep it. That is why `create translations` warns.
--
-- The written element is pinned against a Studio Pro-authored reference
-- (11.13.0, App Settings > Languages > Add):
--
--   {$Type: "Texts$Language", CheckCompleteness: false, Code: "ar_SD",
--    CustomDateFormat: "", CustomDateTimeFormat: "", CustomTimeFormat: ""}
--
-- Note what is NOT there: no Description. Studio Pro's "Arabic, Sudan" is
-- derived from the code for display, so the code is the whole stored identity —
-- which is why these statements take a code and nothing else.

-- 1. Enable a language. Defaults match Studio Pro's Add dialog: completeness
--    unchecked, no custom formats.
alter settings LANGUAGE add 'ar_SD';

-- 2. The same with the options the Edit dialog exposes. CheckCompleteness is a
--    real setting, not cosmetic: with it on, Mendix reports errors and warnings
--    for texts that have no translation in this language, instead of letting them
--    fall back to the default silently.
alter settings LANGUAGE add 'de_DE' (CheckCompleteness: true, CustomDateFormat: 'yyyy-MM-dd');

-- 2b. Change a language that is already enabled. MODIFY touches only the options
--     it names, so turning the completeness check on cannot clear a custom format
--     somebody set in Studio Pro.
alter settings LANGUAGE modify 'ar_SD' (CheckCompleteness: true);

-- 2c. ADD OR MODIFY is the upsert: enables a language that is not there, changes
--     one that is. It is what DESCRIBE emits, which is what makes settings
--     replayable — plain ADD refuses an already-enabled language, so a described
--     project could not be run against itself.
alter settings LANGUAGE add or modify 'ar_SD' (CheckCompleteness: true, CustomDateFormat: 'yyyy-MM-dd');

-- 3. DESCRIBE emits those statements, every option named, with
--    DefaultLanguageCode LAST — the default is validated against the enabled
--    list, so the languages have to exist first. Round-trip measured: replaying
--    the output onto the same project leaves the unit byte-identical and reports
--    `Unchanged language` for each, and replaying it onto a BLANK project
--    reproduces the language settings exactly.
describe settings;

-- 4. Disable one. This REPORTS any translations that become dead weight — it does
--    not refuse. Removing a language does not delete its translations: the
--    enabled list and the translation data are independent, which a stock blank
--    app proves by enabling ONE language while storing translations in EIGHT.
alter settings LANGUAGE remove 'de_DE';

-- 5. Refused: the DEFAULT language. Every missing translation falls back on it,
--    so a project without one has no fallback. Change the default first.
--    alter settings LANGUAGE remove 'en_US';
--
-- 6. NOT refused: a language that still carries translations. The run says how
--    many source strings become dead weight and how to remove them if you want
--    them gone:
--      create or replace translations for ar_SD ( );
